    "result_text": "The suite is green. Here's what I implemented.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe spec: a new owner's `membershipLevel` cannot exceed one above the current maximum among existing household members; with no existing member, no cap. `membershipLevel` was previously computed purely on-the-fly by the mapper, so I made it a snapshot captured at creation (like `householdSize`/`namesakeCount`).\n\n**New pipeline step \u2014 `CapMembershipLevel`** (`rest/function/owner/CapMembershipLevel.java`)\n- Computes the natural level (`MembershipLevel.of`), finds the max *effective* level among existing owners sharing the same `householdId`, and stores `min(natural, maxExisting + 1)`. No household or no existing member \u2192 stores the natural level unchanged.\n- Wired into `owners.POST.yml` after `assignHouseholdSize`/`assignNamesakeCount` (its inputs) and before `save`, under `govern: [ transaction ]`.\n\n**Persistence**\n- Added `membershipLevel` field/column to `Owner` and to all four `schema.sql` files (hsqldb \u2014 the test DB \u2014 h2, mysql, postgres).\n- `MembershipLevel.effective(owner)` returns the stored capped level when present, else the computed value (seed data with no stored level falls back to computed).\n- `OwnerMapper` now maps `membershipLevel` via `effective(...)`, and ignores it on the inbound `OwnerFieldsDto \u2192 Owner` mapping.\n\n**Enabling multi-member households \u2014 `EnsureUniqueIdentity`**\n- The test creates two owners in the same household (same last name + postcode, distinct telephones), but the existing code hard-rejected any second same-household owner with 409 unless `sharesHousehold` was set. That directly contradicted the endpoint's own documented contract \u2014 `IdentityKey`'s javadoc (\"members of the same household with different telephones ... are both allowed\") and the `ensureUniqueIdentity` YAML comment (\"reject 409 **only** when the WHOLE identityKey equals\"). Since the membership-cap rule is defined *over household members*, households must be able to hold multiple members.\n- I aligned the code with that contract: it now rejects only on a whole-`identityKey` collision. `sharesHousehold` still suppresses the soft possible-duplicate flag in `FlagPossibleDuplicate`; I updated the now-stale comment in `AssignHousehold` accordingly.\n\nVerified end-to-end: the audit log shows both owners created with `membershipLevel=1` (first, uncapped) and `membershipLevel=2` (capped at first+1), and `AcceptanceTest` passes \u2014 `BUILD SUCCESS`.\n\nOne design note worth flagging: because the level is now a creation-time snapshot, it no longer changes as an owner's tenure grows across fiscal years. That's consistent with the openapi description (\"assigned on creation\") and the other snapshot fields, and nothing in the spec or tests requires live recomputation \u2014 but say the word if you'd prefer the level stay dynamic.",
